# rules: swift-weak-hash
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-327.swift
# from: CWE-327 (Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm), CWE-328, MASVS-CRYPTO-1
#
# MD5 and SHA-1 are not collision resistant. Producing two inputs with the same
# digest is a laptop-afternoon of work for MD5 and has been done for SHA-1, so
# neither can carry a signature, a token, a password or an integrity check.
# `SHA256` is the same call with a different type name.
#
# CryptoKit says so itself: the namespace is spelled `Insecure`, and the only
# way to reach either algorithm is to type that word. CommonCrypto's `CC_MD5`
# and `CC_SHA1` are the same functions under the older C name, and a lot of
# Swift still calls them through the bridging header.
#
# Naming the types rather than a bare method name is what keeps this rule out
# of the trap the Kotlin pass fell into. There, a pattern that named `md5`
# reported okio's `ByteString.md5()` - a cache key and a certificate pin, both
# real uses of a weak hash and neither of them a weakness. `Insecure.MD5` and
# `CC_MD5` have one meaning each and no other library defines them.
#
# A checksum against accidental corruption is a legitimate use of MD5 and this
# rule reports it. Nothing in the syntax separates it from a signature, and the
# reader can tell in a second where a rule cannot - so the judgement is left
# where it can actually be made.

[ "Insecure.MD5.hash(data: $D)", "Insecure.SHA1.hash(data: $D)",
  "Insecure.MD5.hash($$$_)", "Insecure.SHA1.hash($$$_)",
  "Insecure.MD5()", "Insecure.SHA1()",
  "CC_MD5($$$_)", "CC_SHA1($$$_)" ] as $weak
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $weak)
| finding("swift-weak-hash";
    "this digest is not collision resistant, so it cannot stand for the data "
    + "it was taken of; SHA256 is the same call")
| report
